  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Between Conventional and Experimental is already showing a modest but credible early impact profile: it has 1 OpenAlex citation, 1,472 recorded downloads, and open-access availability through an open repository instance. Its strongest signals point to active use and initial scholarly uptake, alongside thematic relevance to sustainable cities and communities.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published by Leuven University Press in 2024, <em>Between Conventional and Experimental</em> examines mass housing and prefabrication as major forces in modern architecture, with emphasis on Europe and Israel. The book\u2019s subject matter aligns closely with its observed impact pattern: it is being downloaded regularly, it has begun to be cited in scholarly literature, and it carries an SDG alignment with sustainable cities and communities, suggesting relevance to both architectural history and contemporary housing debates.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The most immediate sign of reach is usage. OAPEN reports 1,472 downloads, indicating sustained reading interest rather than a purely archival presence. The monthly record shows continued uptake across the year, which is consistent with a book that is being consulted by readers beyond its initial release period. OpenAIRE also identifies an open instance for the work, supporting discoverability and access.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Scholarly uptake is early but visible. OpenAlex records 1 citation, and the citing work appeared in 2025 in <em>Architecture Structures and Construction</em>. While the citation base is still small, it signals that the book is already entering related research conversations. The citing article\u2019s topics differ from the book\u2019s core subject matter, suggesting that the book\u2019s ideas may be traveling into adjacent applied and engineering-oriented discussions.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The record also shows institutional and funding support from KU Leuven, Sapir College, and the Technion - Israel Institute of Technology. Combined with the book\u2019s open-access status and subject focus on prefabrication, exhibition, and the built environment, these signals suggest a publication positioned for cross-disciplinary relevance. Public event data are unavailable in the current record, so the impact picture here is best read as early scholarly and usage evidence rather than broad public attention.</p>",
